{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013kBcVAKitj1EYjNtQvCABr","type":"tool_result","content":"25\trc.1 tree only. This entry covers the whole train since v1.4.2: everything\n26\tin `[1.4.3]` is part of this release, together with the changes below.\n27\tComing from v1.4.2, read both entries.\n28\t\n29\tThe requirements do not change from v1.4.2: Claude Code 2.1.280 or later,\n30\tPython 3.9 or later, and Node.js 20 or later for the npm shim. `install.sh`,\n31\t`upgrade.sh` and the settings templates are unchanged since v1.4.3-rc.1;\n32\tthe `--harness codex` helper that `install.sh` sources changed (below).\n33\tWhat changes for an install, on top of `[1.4.3]`:\n34\t\n35\t- A new scan in the Bash guard blocks a destructive command that is reached\n36\t  indirectly, on by default (PLAN-195, ADR-201).\n37\t- Several of the largest hooks and scripts become thin entry points over\n38\t  modules under `.claude/hooks/_lib/` (PLAN-197, ADR-202). A hook you\n39\t  patched by hand must be patched again in its module.\n40\t- A registered hook no longer drains the audit spool at exit (a partial\n41\t  cure).\n42\t- The model checks accept model families, among them other vendors' ids\n43\t  and retired ids, and the Codex pin check accepts a newer Codex CLI on a\n44\t  version floor, without a matching hash (Security and governance, below).\n45\t- The secret redactor that the Codex and Grok egress uses no longer cuts\n46\t  the text at offsets taken from its NFKC form, a defect present since\n47\t  v1.0.0. Its `scan()` offsets now index the text passed in (Fixed,\n48\t  below).\n49\t- The kernel's hard-deny list covers the code that moved out of seven\n50\t  kernel entry points, five flat modules that kernel code calls, and the\n51\t  live-adapter package (Fixed, below).\n52\t- Fixes to backup and restore, to `audit-verify-chain.py\n53\t  --strict-against-counter`, to the harness gate, to the plan-lifecycle\n54\t  hook and to the cost-envelope hook, and three findings of the\n55\t  v1.4.3-rc.1 re-pass.\n56\t\n57\tThe honest part: most of this train after v1.4.3-rc.1 reached `main`\n58\tthrough two pull-request merges, without the signing ceremony. They\n59\tchanged 233 canonical paths, 15 of them kernel paths by the list as\n60\tmerged, and with them the meaning of three governance checks: the VETO\n61\tfloor, the model allowlist and the Codex pin. All of this is stated below\n62\tand declared in Known-open, with no release assigned to its cure. Two of\n63\tthe defects this release fixes: the preparation of this release found\n64\tthat the kernel's hard-deny list had not followed the code that moved out\n65\tof seven kernel entry points; and the secret redactor, since v1.0.0, cut\n66\tthe text at offsets taken from its normalized form, which the signed\n67\tv1.4.0 verdict had declared as its condition 58. One condition of the redactor\n68\tfix is declared in Known-open. The spool cure is partial, and the new\n69\tguard's false-positive limits were never measured on this implementation.\n70\tNo speed claim.\n71\t\n72\t**Version number.** This release keeps a PATCH number, 1.4.4, by the\n73\tOwner's decision. `VERSIONING.md` classes an additive SPEC change (the two\n74\taudit-log enums below) and a new trust boundary as MINOR, and this train\n75\thas both: the indirect-execution guard on by default, and the model checks\n76\tand Codex pin above. Plan an upgrade from v1.4.2 as you would plan a MINOR\n77\tone.\n78\t\n79\t### Added — a Bash guard against indirect execution, on by default (PLAN-195, ADR-201)\n80\t\n81\t- After its existing checks, `check_bash_safety.py` runs one bounded scan\n82\t  (`_lib/bash_indirect_guard.py`). The scan looks for `rm -rf`,\n83\t  `git reset --hard` or a forced `git push` reached indirectly:\n84\t  - inside `sh -c`, `eval` or a here-doc;\n85\t  - behind a known launcher;\n86\t  - through a computed command name;\n87\t  - in a program fed by a pipe, a redirect, a descriptor or a process\n88\t    substitution.\n89\t- It also blocks a computed write when it resolves the target from the\n90\t  command text to a canonical path.\n91\t- It only adds blocks; an earlier BLOCK stays BLOCK. Its reason codes are\n92\t  `bash_indirect_*` and `bash_computed_canonical_target`.\n93\t- It fails closed on a recognized shell body it cannot parse, and at its\n94\t  depth, size and word limits.\n95\t- Two switches control it. Both are on unless set to `0` in the\n96\t  environment Claude Code starts with:\n97\t  - `CEO_BASH_INDIRECT_GUARD=0` turns off the scan;\n98\t  - `CEO_BASH_INDIRECT_HEURISTICS=0` turns off only the computed-verb and\n99\t    opaque-feeder forms.\n100\t- An explicit disable writes at most one `learning_rail_disabled` event per\n101\t  session and switch.\n102\t\n103\t### Changed — `SPEC/v1/audit-log.schema.md`: two closed enums gain values\n104\t\n105\t- `learning_rail_disabled.rail` gains `bash_indirect`, and `.switch` gains\n106\t  the two switch names above. No event and no field is added. A consumer\n107\t  that pins the old enum lists rejects the new values.\n108\t- The row's prose still calls ADR-201 `PROPOSED`; the ADR is `ACCEPTED`.\n109\t\n110\t### Changed — hooks and scripts split into modules (PLAN-197, ADR-202)\n111\t\n112\t- Entry points keep their paths and their observable contracts. Their\n113\t  implementations move into packages under `.claude/hooks/_lib/`. For\n114\t  example, `_lib/audit_emit.py` goes from 14,522 lines to 496, and"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"34b005ba-8b89-4e2d-a38f-79b02c6d7b54","uuid":"fc2f0729-3159-46cf-b334-34b072cb1125","timestamp":"2026-11-13T17:28:48.186Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/var/folders/9n/hm9srl0s1nz45_p12tj61pd80000gn/T/kit-repass-tree.nUJTgA/tree/CHANGELOG.md","content":"rc.1 tree only. This entry covers the whole train since v1.4.2: everything\nin `[1.4.3]` is part of this release, together with the changes below.\nComing from v1.4.2, read both entries.\n\nThe requirements do not change from v1.4.2: Claude Code 2.1.280 or later,\nPython 3.9 or later, and Node.js 20 or later for the npm shim. `install.sh`,\n`upgrade.sh` and the settings templates are unchanged since v1.4.3-rc.1;\nthe `--harness codex` helper that `install.sh` sources changed (below).\nWhat changes for an install, on top of `[1.4.3]`:\n\n- A new scan in the Bash guard blocks a destructive command that is reached\n  indirectly, on by default (PLAN-195, ADR-201).\n- Several of the largest hooks and scripts become thin entry points over\n  modules under `.claude/hooks/_lib/` (PLAN-197, ADR-202). A hook you\n  patched by hand must be patched again in its module.\n- A registered hook no longer drains the audit spool at exit (a partial\n  cure).\n- The model checks accept model families, among them other vendors' ids\n  and retired ids, and the Codex pin check accepts a newer Codex CLI on a\n  version floor, without a matching hash (Security and governance, below).\n- The secret redactor that the Codex and Grok egress uses no longer cuts\n  the text at offsets taken from its NFKC form, a defect present since\n  v1.0.0. Its `scan()` offsets now index the text passed in (Fixed,\n  below).\n- The kernel's hard-deny list covers the code that moved out of seven\n  kernel entry points, five flat modules that kernel code calls, and the\n  live-adapter package (Fixed, below).\n- Fixes to backup and restore, to `audit-verify-chain.py\n  --strict-against-counter`, to the harness gate, to the plan-lifecycle\n  hook and to the cost-envelope hook, and three findings of the\n  v1.4.3-rc.1 re-pass.\n\nThe honest part: most of this train after v1.4.3-rc.1 reached `main`\nthrough two pull-request merges, without the signing ceremony. They\nchanged 233 canonical paths, 15 of them kernel paths by the list as\nmerged, and with them the meaning of three governance checks: the VETO\nfloor, the model allowlist and the Codex pin. All of this is stated below\nand declared in Known-open, with no release assigned to its cure. Two of\nthe defects this release fixes: the preparation of this release found\nthat the kernel's hard-deny list had not followed the code that moved out\nof seven kernel entry points; and the secret redactor, since v1.0.0, cut\nthe text at offsets taken from its normalized form, which the signed\nv1.4.0 verdict had declared as its condition 58. One condition of the redactor\nfix is declared in Known-open. The spool cure is partial, and the new\nguard's false-positive limits were never measured on this implementation.\nNo speed claim.\n\n**Version number.** This release keeps a PATCH number, 1.4.4, by the\nOwner's decision. `VERSIONING.md` classes an additive SPEC change (the two\naudit-log enums below) and a new trust boundary as MINOR, and this train\nhas both: the indirect-execution guard on by default, and the model checks\nand Codex pin above. Plan an upgrade from v1.4.2 as you would plan a MINOR\none.\n\n### Added — a Bash guard against indirect execution, on by default (PLAN-195, ADR-201)\n\n- After its existing checks, `check_bash_safety.py` runs one bounded scan\n  (`_lib/bash_indirect_guard.py`). The scan looks for `rm -rf`,\n  `git reset --hard` or a forced `git push` reached indirectly:\n  - inside `sh -c`, `eval` or a here-doc;\n  - behind a known launcher;\n  - through a computed command name;\n  - in a program fed by a pipe, a redirect, a descriptor or a process\n    substitution.\n- It also blocks a computed write when it resolves the target from the\n  command text to a canonical path.\n- It only adds blocks; an earlier BLOCK stays BLOCK. Its reason codes are\n  `bash_indirect_*` and `bash_computed_canonical_target`.\n- It fails closed on a recognized shell body it cannot parse, and at its\n  depth, size and word limits.\n- Two switches control it. Both are on unless set to `0` in the\n  environment Claude Code starts with:\n  - `CEO_BASH_INDIRECT_GUARD=0` turns off the scan;\n  - `CEO_BASH_INDIRECT_HEURISTICS=0` turns off only the computed-verb and\n    opaque-feeder forms.\n- An explicit disable writes at most one `learning_rail_disabled` event per\n  session and switch.\n\n### Changed — `SPEC/v1/audit-log.schema.md`: two closed enums gain values\n\n- `learning_rail_disabled.rail` gains `bash_indirect`, and `.switch` gains\n  the two switch names above. No event and no field is added. A consumer\n  that pins the old enum lists rejects the new values.\n- The row's prose still calls ADR-201 `PROPOSED`; the ADR is `ACCEPTED`.\n\n### Changed — hooks and scripts split into modules (PLAN-197, ADR-202)\n\n- Entry points keep their paths and their observable contracts. Their\n  implementations move into packages under `.claude/hooks/_lib/`. For\n  example, `_lib/audit_emit.py` goes from 14,522 lines to 496, and","numLines":90,"startLine":25,"totalLines":2658}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_013kBcVAKitj1EYjNtQvCABr","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
